  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 142 PM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nWednesday through Monday:\n\nA shortwave trough tracking across the Northern Plains will reach\nWI by mid Wednesday morning. A cluster of thunderstorms is\nexpected with this shortwave, so there is uncertainty about the\ntrack of this area of storms. Will they track straight across\nsouthern WI as they stay tied to the upper jet? Or will they dive\nsouthward with the nose of the LLJ, 850mb frontal boundary, and\nthe low-level instability gradient? This will depend on how\norganized they become upstream during the early Wed morning \nhours. The trend is for the storms to split around southern WI,\nthus we only have low chances (20 to 35%) of storms. \n\nOn Thursday, yet another shortwave is expected to swing across\nMN/IA/WI/IL, which will bring another round of storms, mainly\nalong and south of the WI/IL border. Northeast winds will push all\nthe way through southern WI by Friday in the wake of a more\nprominent shortwave trough that crosses Lake Superior. Friday\nhighs will be in the mid-upper 70s and cooler along the lakeshore. \n\nThe pattern will start shift a little as we get into the weekend.\nThe mid level high pressure that has been parked over the\nsouthwest and south central states is expected to shift more\ntoward the southeast states. This would temporarily allow a more\namplified wave and upper jet to cross the Northern Plains/Great\nLakes. In turn, this would help usher the low level jet farther\nnorth toward central WI and bring a chance for stronger storms \nto southern WI. The timing of this looks like Saturday night \ninto Sunday morning. Of course, this is a long way out and \nthings will change, but the models are starting to latch on to \nthis idea. \n\nHigh pressure in the wake of this system should bring a period of\nquiet weather for the first half of next week. \n\nCronce",
